        HelpPage(slug: "animation", title: "Animating objects and text", group: "Web and animation", summary: "WireTuner animates frame by frame, with each frame drawn on its own layer or its own page.  There are no tweens and no timeline of keyframes; instead you build the frames as artwork -- often by blending two shapes and releasing the blend to layers -- and the document's",
                 headings: ["How frames are built", "Releasing objects to layers", "Animation settings and preview", "Exporting an animation", "Working with others"],
                 keywords: ["frame", "layers", "animation", "layer", "choose", "panel", "document", "first", "frames", "blend", "every", "piece", "pieces", "shows", "release", "export", "animated", "select", "step", "releasing", "play", "loop", "group", "objects", "trail", "current", "format", "option", "browser", "same", "settings", "file", "foreground", "motion", "hold", "block", "window", "mode", "cols", "click"]),
        HelpPage(slug: "applying-color", title: "Applying color to objects", group: "Color", summary: "Color reaches an object in one of four ways: you drag it there from a panel, you pick it in the Tools panel's color wells, you choose it in the Object panel for a particular fill or stroke, or you lift it off another object with the Eyedropper.  All four write the same thing -- a fill color or a stroke color -- so use whichever is under your hand.  Colors come from the Color Mixer, the Tints panel and the Swatches panel; the difference between a color you applied from the Mixer and one you applied from the Swatches list is that the second is named and follows the swatch when the swatch is edited.",
