        HelpPage(slug: "attribute-stack", title: "Strokes, fills and the Attributes list", group: "Strokes and fills", summary: "Every object in WireTuner carries a list of attributes: the strokes that outline it, the fills that color its interior, and the effects that alter how those look.  An object can have several of each, in any order, and the order is part of the design: a narrow white stroke above a wide black one makes a keyline; a translucent gradient above a solid fill makes a sheen.  The Object panel is where you build and rearrange that list.  This page covers the list itself; the pages that follow cover what each stroke, fill and effect can be (Strokes, Fills, Gradients, Live effects).",
                 headings: ["The Attributes list", "Applying color", "Default attributes", "Working with others"],
                 keywords: ["list", "object", "stroke", "attribute", "fill", "attributes", "panel", "color", "selected", "above", "same", "effects", "effect", "edit", "fills", "select", "drawn", "shows", "default", "every", "strokes", "part", "objects", "choose", "action", "menu", "top-most", "visible", "several", "order", "gradient", "bottom", "item", "kind", "basic", "click", "nothing", "removing", "remove", "settings"]),
        HelpPage(slug: "bitmaps", title: "Working with bitmap images", group: "Imported artwork", summary: "A bitmap image in a WireTuner document is an object like any other: it has a position, a size, a rotation, a name, and a place in a layer's stacking order.  What makes it different is that it is made of pixels rather than paths, so its quality depends on how many pixels it has for the size it is printed at.  This page covers what the Object panel shows and lets you change for an image, how scaling affects its resolution, how transparency, alpha channels and tinting work, and how WireTuner displays large images.  Cropping is on Cropping bitmap images, and editing pixels is on Editing images in other applications.",
                 headings: ["Image properties in the Object panel", "Resizing, rotating and skewing images", "Alpha channels and transparency", "Bilevel and grayscale images", "Removing a background", "Displaying images", "Working with others"],
                 keywords: ["image", "pixels", "object", "images", "panel", "color", "resolution", "alpha", "background", "document", "grayscale", "transparent", "size", "channel", "ramp", "path", "shows", "effective", "gray", "wiretuner", "edit", "subject", "transparency", "file", "without", "scale", "keeps", "sheet", "settings", "another", "same", "fill", "mode", "transform", "setting", "print", "white", "choose", "every", "drawn"]),
        HelpPage(slug: "blends", title: "Working with blends", group: "Effects", summary: "A blend is a series of in-between shapes drawn from one object to another: a small red circle blending into a large blue square passes through shapes that are a little larger, a little squarer and a little bluer at each step.  Blends make smooth color transitions, contour-like shading, and rows of evolving shapes.  In WireTuner blends are live: the objects you blended stay inside the blend, and editing one of them -- its shape, its colors, its stroke -- redraws every intermediate step.  A blend can also be joined to a path so the steps follow a curve.",
                 headings: ["What can be blended", "Using the Blend tool", "Blending from the menu", "Adjusting a blend in the Object panel", "Joining a blend to a path", "Releasing a blend", "Working with others"],
                 keywords: ["blend", "path", "steps", "object", "objects", "paths", "point", "blends", "tool", "select", "choose", "intermediate", "same", "first", "modify", "spot", "color", "colors", "composite", "groups", "direction", "drag", "change", "step", "stroke", "joined", "fill", "order", "ends", "both", "become", "edit", "shapes", "shape", "process", "panel", "last", "start", "original", "another"]),
        HelpPage(slug: "branches", title: "Branches", group: "Collaboration", summary: "A branch is a place to try something without changing the document everyone else is working in.  It starts as an exact copy of the document at the moment you create it, you work in it exactly as you would in the document, and when you are done you merge it back -- all of it, or object by object -- through the same review you use after working offline.  Branches are for \"let me try a new palette for a week\", for \"I want to rework this page without anyone tripping over it\", and for keeping the changes you made offline when you would rather not merge them yet (Working together).",
                 headings: ["What a branch is", "Creating a branch", "Switching between the parent and its branches", "Seeing what changed", "Merging a branch", "Archiving and deleting a branch", "Branch permissions", "Branches while offline", "Working with others"],
                 keywords: ["branch", "parent", "branches", "merge", "changes", "choose", "document", "offline", "window", "same", "file", "working", "main", "sheet", "merged", "create", "object", "title", "popup", "merging", "library", "review", "open", "created", "compare", "changed", "keep", "without", "work", "palette", "people", "history", "network", "objects", "both", "since", "marked", "merges", "server", "copy"]),
        HelpPage(slug: "charts", title: "Charts and pictographs", group: "Drawing", summary: "The Chart tool turns a small table of numbers into a column, line, pie, area or scatter chart drawn with ordinary WireTuner objects.  The chart remembers its data: reopen the Chart sheet at any time to change the numbers or the chart type and the drawing updates.  You can recolor and restyle the bars, lines and wedges, replace columns with pictures (pictographs), and, when you want to take the drawing apart by hand, ungroup the chart into plain paths and text.  Charts are drawn in grays; color them afterwards with the usual tools.",
                 headings: ["Creating a chart", "Chart types and options", "Styling chart elements", "Pictographs", "Ungrouping a chart", "Working with others"],
                 keywords: ["chart", "column", "data", "click", "sheet", "type", "choose", "cell", "axis", "columns", "value", "line", "copy", "tool", "scatter", "grouped", "across", "marks", "values", "apply", "edit", "select", "series", "numbers", "preview", "grid", "label", "legend", "right", "cells", "artwork", "area", "drawn", "drawing", "recolor", "replace", "plain", "charts", "drag", "opens"]),
        HelpPage(slug: "clipping-paths", title: "Clipping paths", group: "Objects", summary: "A clipping path is a closed path that shows only what lies inside it.  Paste artwork, text or a bitmap into a path and everything beyond the path's edge is hidden -- not cut away.  The contents stay whole, and you can edit, move and transform them inside the clip at any time.",
                 headings: ["Creating a clipping path", "The Object panel", "Editing the contents", "Transforming a clipping path", "Working with others"],
                 keywords: ["path", "contents", "clipping", "paste", "edit", "inside", "shape", "handle", "select", "choose", "subselect", "object", "objects", "becomes", "everything", "move", "transform", "clip", "fill", "behind", "without", "again", "clipboard", "unclipped", "release", "closed", "shows", "bitmap", "pasted", "shift", "stroke", "selected", "points", "edits", "double-click", "moving", "panel", "canvas", "drag", "delete"]),
        HelpPage(slug: "collaboration", title: "Working together", group: "Collaboration", summary: "A WireTuner document is never \"checked out\".  Everyone who has access to it can open it at the same time, and everyone edits the same document: the shape you drag, the color your colleague picks and the headline a third person retypes all land in one place, and each of you sees the others' work within a moment of it happening.  There is no Save button that sends work to the cloud -- every edit is saved and shared as you make it (Saving) -- and there are no locks to wait for.  This page is the overview: what you see when other people are in the document, what happens when two of you touch the same thing, and how WireTuner handles the work you did while you were offline.  The details are on their own pages: Presence, Comments, Sharing, Branches, Version history and Inspect mode.",
                 headings: ["What multiplayer means here", "Who is here", "Seeing others work", "When someone deletes what you have selected", "When two people change the same thing", "Locked objects", "Working offline", "The Review Changes sheet", "Roles at a glance"],
                 keywords: ["object", "same", "changes", "document", "both", "change", "else", "changed", "theirs", "version", "history", "someone", "attribute", "review", "panel", "edits", "person", "work", "offline", "collaborator", "merge", "later", "open", "people", "keep", "everything", "deleted", "yours", "wiretuner", "copy", "objects", "nothing", "name", "selected", "edited", "sheet", "everyone", "others", "sends", "every"]),
        HelpPage(slug: "color-management", title: "Color management", group: "Color management", summary: "Color management is how WireTuner keeps the colors you see on screen honest about the colors that will come out of a press, a proof printer, or someone else's display.  A monitor makes color by adding red, green and blue light; a press makes it by laying cyan, magenta, yellow and black ink on paper, and the press can reach far fewer colors than the screen.  Every device in between -- a scanner, a camera, a colleague's laptop, a proofer -- has its own idea of what a given color number means.  A color management system reads a profile for each device (a mathematical description of the colors that device can produce) and translates between them, so that the swatch you mixed looks as close as the hardware allows to what will finally be printed or shown.",
                 headings: ["A color-managed workflow", "The Color Settings sheet", "Where each color goes", "About rendering intent", "Proofing colors on screen", "Preferences that stay on your Mac", "Working with others"],
                 keywords: ["color", "profile", "colors", "display", "cmyk", "proof", "document", "working", "screen", "settings", "press", "intent", "proofing", "artwork", "default", "space", "image", "change", "every", "spot", "srgb", "choose", "device", "source", "shown", "same", "file", "profiles", "output", "view", "printer", "black", "paper", "proofer", "rendering", "sheet", "separations", "composite", "white", "changes"]),
        HelpPage(slug: "color-mixer", title: "Using the Color Mixer panel", group: "Color", summary: "The Color Mixer is where colors are made.  Pick a mode, move the sliders or type numbers, watch the color well, and when it is right either apply it directly -- as an unnamed color -- or add it to the Swatches panel as a named color.  It is also the place to inspect a color: drop any swatch or object color on it and the sliders show the components.  Show or hide the panel with Window Color Mixer.",
                 headings: ["The panel", "Mixing a color", "Inspecting a color", "Working with others"],
                 keywords: ["color", "panel", "mixer", "display", "colors", "well", "swatches", "sliders", "oklch", "shows", "swatch", "show", "srgb", "space", "field", "screen", "lightness", "mode", "apply", "slider", "gamut", "object", "cmyk", "drag", "indicator", "click", "type", "half", "same", "pick", "right", "change", "current", "stroke", "chroma", "near", "blue", "black", "components", "split"]),
        HelpPage(slug: "color-profiles", title: "Choosing color profiles", group: "Color management", summary: "A profile is a small file that describes a device's color behavior: which colors it can reproduce and how its numeric values map to colors the eye can see.  Profiles follow the International Color Consortium (ICC) standard; WireTuner reads ICC version 2 and version 4 profiles.  Manufacturers ship profiles for their displays, printers and paper stocks; standards bodies publish profiles for common press conditions; and calibration software builds custom profiles by measuring a device.  A custom profile measured on your own device is always more accurate than a generic one.",
                 headings: ["Which profile goes where", "Choosing a profile", "Managing the document's profiles", "Profiles on export and print", "Working with others"],
                 keywords: ["profile", "profiles", "working", "cmyk", "color", "document", "colors", "file", "display", "export", "srgb", "device", "composite", "images", "press", "output", "space", "default", "printer", "menu", "choose", "separations", "printing", "slot", "lists", "standard", "wiretuner", "version", "bundled", "installed", "chosen", "proof", "settings", "convert", "wide-gamut", "image", "exporting", "small", "paper", "standards"]),
        HelpPage(slug: "color-tables", title: "Color conversion and spot color preview", group: "Color management", summary: "Every time WireTuner shows a CMYK swatch on an RGB screen, previews a PANTONE color, or fills in the RGB equivalent of a CMYK mix in the Color Mixer, it converts between color spaces.  This page explains where those conversions come from, why every collaborator gets the same answer, and how spot colors are previewed.",
                 headings: ["How conversions are computed", "How spot colors are previewed", "Getting the same color on two Macs", "Working with others"],
                 keywords: ["color", "cmyk", "spot", "document", "mixer", "working", "display", "profile", "values", "swatch", "same", "colors", "conversion", "every", "wiretuner", "computed", "chip", "intent", "proof", "measured", "process", "screen", "manage", "profiles", "management", "settings", "numbers", "library", "press", "value", "printed", "time", "shows", "pantone", "converts", "conversions", "come", "collaborator", "previewed", "needs"]),
        HelpPage(slug: "columns-tables", title: "Columns, rows and tables", group: "Type", summary: "A text block can be divided into columns, rows, or both.  Columns are the usual way to set a long text in a narrow measure; columns and rows together make a grid of equal cells -- a simple table or a form -- and the block's stroke draws the lines between them.  Tables with cells of different widths are built with tabs instead (xref:tabs-indents.adoc[Tabs, indents and margins]).  The Adjust Columns section then helps the text fill its columns evenly.",
                 headings: ["Creating columns and rows", "Tables", "Balancing and fitting columns", "Linked blocks and columns", "Working with others"],
